WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.990
On results day, the student portal takes

2
00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:05.370
eight seconds to show one student's

3
00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:05.590
grades.

4
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.820
The query is one line.

5
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.500
After a single change, it

6
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.660
takes three milliseconds.

7
00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:13.310
What changed?

8
00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.610
Today: why a query on a million rows

9
00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.930
can take seconds or milliseconds, how

10
00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.830
B-tree indexes work, and how to teach

11
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.650
students to read a query plan.

12
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.560
An index is a separate structure that

13
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.000
maps key values to row locations.

14
00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.970
Without one, the database scans every row.

15
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.660
With one, it jumps almost

16
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.280
straight to the rows it needs.

17
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:44.510
A B-tree keeps keys sorted in wide

18
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.910
nodes, each holding hundreds of keys.

19
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.500
Because the tree is so wide,

20
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.340
it stays very shallow.

21
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.680
A million rows need only three or four

22
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.220
levels, so three or four page reads.

23
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.020
The portal filtered grades by student ID,

24
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.400
but only the grade ID was indexed.

25
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.490
Every request scanned the whole table.

26
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.900
One create index statement fixed it.

27
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.560
Every index speeds some reads but slows

28
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.220
every write, because it must be updated

29
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:18.600
too.

30
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.440
It also takes space.

31
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.280
And an index on a column with only

32
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.160
two values rarely helps, since the

33
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.360
planner will scan anyway.

34
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.420
Students often think indexes are magic

35
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.080
or that more is always better.

36
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.280
Give them a large table and have them run

37
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.380
EXPLAIN before and after adding an index.

38
00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.650
Measuring both read and write time

39
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.330
makes the trade-off concrete.

40
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:51.240
Quick check!

41
00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.960
A B-tree holds about two

42
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.180
hundred keys per node.

43
00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.550
Roughly how many levels does it

44
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.430
need for eight million rows?

45
00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.830
Three: two hundred cubed is eight million.

46
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.730
That is why indexed lookups stay fast as

47
00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.670
tables grow by orders of magnitude.

48
00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.230
In the app, open the index lab.

49
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.970
Run the same lookup with and

50
00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.150
without an index and count how

51
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.190
many rows each method touches.

52
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.110
Your turn.

53
00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.450
A column holds only yes or

54
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.270
no, evenly split.

55
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.620
Will an index on it usually speed

56
00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.400
up a filter on that column?

57
00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.620
Usually not: half the rows

58
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.580
match, so scanning is cheaper.

59
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.800
Remember: B-trees stay shallow, every

60
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.580
index costs writes and space, and EXPLAIN

61
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.100
shows what the planner decided.

62
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.540
Try it in your next lecture: load

63
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.140
a million-row table and let pairs

64
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.720
run one slow query.

65
00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:59.390
They read the plan, add one

66
00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.130
index and run it again.

67
00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.490
Then they measure how much

68
00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.170
slower inserts became.
